\chapter{Typed quotation and staged execution}
\label{ch:40}
\index{Typed quotation and staged execution}
\index{Staging!quotation worlds}

A typed quote represents a bounded later-stage computation. \OnxCodeTerm{lift} embeds an admitted present-stage value, and a splice composes compatible code inside a quote. Lexical quotation regions can contain local statements, cleanup and a tail expression. The checker tracks quotation-world identity as well as depth, so two siblings at equal depth cannot use each other's local binders.

\section{Follow the generated executable}
The compiler lowers the supported profile to AOT factories and typed thunks. A runtime action invokes the generated code. The running program does not parse a string or call a JIT. Compare a quoted arithmetic example with a normal closure produced by a factory. Observe which operands the staging rules capture, and keep a rejected cross-world binding as a negative test. Operations with restricted ownership, foreign effects, or unsupported staging transport require their own profiles. \OnxCodeTerm{--emit-ast} and emitted C answer different questions about where a stage boundary disappears.

\section{Predict the staging transcript}
The staging example uses a function \OnxCodeTerm{sample} which prints its argument and returns it. This lets us observe the difference between saving an expression for later and saving the result of evaluating it now. Cover the last part of the program and consider just the construction of the two operands of \OnxCodeTerm{add}. The left quotation contains a local initialized by calling \OnxCodeTerm{sample(40)}. That statement belongs to the quoted region, so constructing this operand has not printed 40. In the right quotation the call with 2 is the operand of a lift. The code value retains its result; evaluating the lift input accounts for a print of 2 before the program announces construction.

The combining function splices the two operands into a sum. Running the combined value executes the left region, producing the marker 40, and uses the saved 2 on the right. The enclosing print reports 42. Running it again produces another 40 and another 42, but no second marker for 2. A trace with two identical final sums but two prints of 2 would indicate a different division of work between stages.

Finally read the type of the nested quote from the outside in. Its first run yields another code value; the second run obtains the lifted integer 42. Counting pairs of braces is less useful than asking, at each run, whether the result is already a number or still a computation that produces one.

It is worth keeping the construction marker in this experiment. Without it, a trace showing one print of 2 would distinguish repeated from single evaluation but would not locate that evaluation relative to construction.

